approaches.ideaEn:
Keep the stack decreasing from bottom to top so the top is the minimum; `pop` and `peek` then work on the top directly. Only `push v` needs care: v must go to its sorted place.

The book uses one temporary stack: pop every element smaller than v from the main stack into the temp stack, push v, then pour the temp stack back. The main stack stays sorted at O(n) per push in the worst case.

In Python a list can do this with binary search and `insort`: store `-v` in ascending order so the end of the list (the top) holds the smallest v. The shift is a memory move, much faster than popping and pushing element by element.

- `push v`: `insort(stack, -v)`;
- `pop`: `stack.pop()` if non-empty, otherwise do nothing;
- `peek`: print `-stack[-1]`, or -1 if empty;
- `isEmpty`: whether the list is empty.

**Pitfalls**: pop removes the minimum, not the most recent push; pop on an empty stack prints nothing.

