The same structure as a regular expression:

`[+-]?(\d+\.?\d*|\.\d+)([eE][+-]?\d+)?`

- `\d+\.?\d*` covers numbers with digits left of the point: `42`, `3.`, `3.14`;
- `\.\d+` covers no digits left of the point but some on the right: `.5`;
- together the two branches rule out a lone `.`.

Use `fullmatch` so the whole string must match, not just a prefix. In Python 3 `\d` also matches non-ASCII digits; the input here is ASCII, but `[0-9]` is stricter. The regex is short, yet interviews often ask for the hand-written automaton, so know both.

